7 Text Replacements
*******************

Speech recognition makes the same mistakes again and again.  It writes
"settle cassin" for zettelkasten and leaves every contraction in place.
When ‘whisper-dvr-replacements-file’ names the CSV file that the
Voice-In Plus Chrome extension exports, whisper-dvr fixes these errors
in each transcript before it is inserted and before the LLM sees it.
The first column of the file holds the trigger and the second the
replacement.

   Think of the step as a proofreader with a phrase book.  The
proofreader reads one word at a time, tries the longest phrase in the
book first, and never rereads a correction, so one replacement cannot
set off another.  Whitespace and the punctuation around words are kept.

7.1 Tiers
=========

A Voice-In table mixes corrections with dictation commands.  A command
fires only when it is said on purpose, but a recording holds whatever
was said.  So each rule is sorted into a tier, and only the tiers in
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-tiers’ are applied.

‘contraction’
     A contraction such as ‘can't’.  Applied by default.
‘correction’
     A misheard word or a proper noun.  Applied by default.
‘expansion’
     A spoken abbreviation whose trigger starts with a cue word such as
     ‘expand’.  Applied only when listed.
‘command’
     A rule that inserts markup, a template, or text with formatting
     codes.  Never applied.
‘action’
     A rule that opens a web page or presses a key.  Never applied.
‘template’
     A rule whose replacement spans several lines.  Never applied.

   A deny list in ‘whisper-dvr-replacements-deny-file’ removes
corrections whose trigger is an ordinary word, such as ‘may’.  The
script ‘audit_rules.py’ in the Claude skill proposes this list from
English word frequencies, and ‘make skill-data’ runs it.

7.2 Contractions
================

When ‘whisper-dvr-replacements-context’ is non-nil, a contraction that
ends in ‘'s’ or ‘'d’ is read from the next word, using the rules of
expand-contractions.el.  "I'd like" becomes "I would like", "I'd never
seen" becomes "I had never seen", and "he's been" becomes "he has been".
When LLM post-processing follows, the default value ‘when-llm’ of
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-skip-ambiguous’ leaves these forms for the LLM
instead.

   The ‘contractions.csv’ table from expand-contractions.el can be
listed in ‘whisper-dvr-replacements-extra-files’.  A later file
overrides an earlier one, and its header line is skipped.

     (let ((data "~/6112MooersLabGitHubLabRepos/whisper-dvr/skills/voice-in-replacements/data/"))
       (setq whisper-dvr-replacements-file (concat data "replacements.csv")
             whisper-dvr-replacements-extra-files
             (list (concat data "contractions.csv")
                   (concat data "whisper-extra.csv"))
             whisper-dvr-replacements-deny-file (concat data "deny.txt")))

7.3 Replacement commands
========================

These commands work on any text, not only on DVR transcripts.  Each
change is one undo step.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-apply-region’
     Apply the replacements to the selected text.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-apply-buffer’
     Apply the replacements to the whole buffer, or to the accessible
     part of a narrowed buffer.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-apply-file’
     Read a file such as ‘notes.txt’ and write ‘notes-replaced.txt’
     beside it.  The original is left alone.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-reload’
     Reread the rule files.  The files are also reread whenever they
     change.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-audit’
     Show the tier counts, the number of active rules, and the problems
     found in the rule files.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-show-log’
     Show the raw text and the replacements of each pass.

   ‘whisper-dvr-llm-process-region’ and ‘whisper-dvr-llm-process-file’
apply the replacements before they call the LLM unless
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-before-llm-commands’ is ‘nil’.

7.4 The Claude skill
====================

The folder ‘skills/voice-in-replacements’ holds a Claude skill with a
Python copy of the same engine, for text that never passes through
Emacs.  ‘make skill-zip’ packages it.  Both engines pass the cases in
‘tests/replacement-cases.tsv’ of the skill.

7.5 Replacement settings
========================

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-enabled’
     Non-nil applies the rules to new transcripts.  Default ‘t’.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-file’
     The Voice-In Plus CSV export.  Nothing happens while it is ‘nil’.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-extra-files’
     Later rule files, each overriding the files before it.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-deny-file’
     Triggers that must never fire, one per line.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-tiers’
     The tiers that are applied.  Default ‘(contraction correction)’.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-context’
     Non-nil reads ‘'s’ and ‘'d’ contractions from the next word.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-has-triggers’
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-had-triggers’
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-ed-base-verbs’
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-skippable-words’
     The word lists of the context rules.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-skip-ambiguous’
     ‘when-llm’ leaves ambiguous contractions for the LLM when it runs,
     ‘t’ always leaves them, and ‘nil’ never does.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-before-llm-commands’
     Non-nil applies the rules in the LLM region and file commands.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-log’
‘whisper-dvr-replacements-log-file’
     Where each pass is recorded with its raw text.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-date-format’
     Format of the date that the ‘<date>’ code inserts.

‘whisper-dvr-replacements-llm-note’
     A note added to the LLM instructions while replacements are active.


